# Dimensionality Reduction & Feature Selection in Python

This repository documents hands-on exercises and code snippets exploring the **curse of dimensionality**, train-test data splitting, model evaluation using Support Vector Machines (SVM), and feature selection techniques using the ANSUR dataset.

## 🚀 Key Concepts & Workflow

### 1. The Curse of Dimensionality
* **The Problem:** High-dimensional datasets (many features) often cause models to **overfit**, memorizing the training data instead of learning general patterns.
* **The Solution:** Apply dimensionality reduction or feature selection to reduce noise and improve model generalization on unseen data.

### 2. Model Training & Evaluation Workflow
1. **Train-Test Split:** Splitting the dataset into training and testing sets (typically 70% train / 30% test) to evaluate performance objectively.
2. **Baseline Model:** Training a Support Vector Classifier (`SVC()`) on the full feature set.
3. **Dimensionality Reduction:** Restricting features to high-value columns (e.g., `'neckcircumferencebase'`) to combat overfitting and maintain high predictive accuracy.
4. **Variance Thresholds:** Removing low-variance features that provide little to no distinguishing information using scikit-learn's `VarianceThreshold`.

